    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\), we can use the property of inverses, which states that \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = (\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A})^{-1}\\). Using the property \\((\\mathbf{AB})^{-1} = \\mathbf{B}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\), we get that \\((\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A})^{-1} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\). The right hand side is the identity matrix that we're multiplying \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\) with, so we get \\(\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}\\), the identity matrix.\n\nGiven that \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can find \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) by taking the transpose of \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\). The transpose of a matrix is obtained by swapping the rows and columns of the matrix. So, \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is in the form \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Now, we compute \\(\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nPerforming the matrix multiplication, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 5 & 2 \\cdot (-1) + 5 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ -1 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot 5 & -1 \\cdot (-1) + (-3) \\cdot (-3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 + 25 & -2 - 15 \\\\ -2 - 15 & 1 + 9 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 29 & -17 \\\\ -17 & 10 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThis multiplication results in the identity matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Therefore, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is \\(\\mathbf{A}\\), which is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nHere's the final result:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify the result using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the inverse of A\r\nA_inv = sp.Matrix([[2, 5], [-1, -3]])\r\n\r\n# Compute the inverse of A^2, which is A*A_inv\r\nA_inv_result = A_inv * A_inv\r\n\r\n# Simplify the result\r\nA_inv_result = sp.simplify(A_inv_result)\r\nprint(A_inv_result)\n```",
